// ------------------------------------------------------------
//  Rozdział dziesiąty — Narodziny (mamuny). Pierwsza zima po weselu,
//  Gromniczna (gromnica), przestrogi babki Hanki, narodziny Marysi pod
//  koniec sierpnia (Jaromir ma dwadzieścia lat). Ochrona kołyski:
//  wstążka, siekiera dziadka albo gromnica. Trzecia noc: mamuny —
//  zasłonić kołyskę, zawołać Hankę (z krzyżykiem kowala budzi się)
//  albo pogonić je siekierą. Podmiana: skorupki z księgą odwracają ją
//  od razu, rozstaje i chrzest odmieńca prowadzą nad jezioro, gdzie
//  trzeba zapłacić: skarbem z dzieciństwa, darem świętojańskim albo
//  własnymi latami. Skarb może trafić do kołyski Marysi.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

=== rozdzial_dziesiaty ===
# tlo: izba_zimowa
# rozdzial: Chapter Ten
# tytul: The Birth
Hanka moved into the new room with a single chest, two featherbeds and a spinning wheel from her mother. Within a week Jaromir’s mother was calling her “our Hanka”, and within a month she had stopped checking whether her daughter-in-law had set the bread dough properly.
Snow fell even before St Andrew’s Eve and lay until March. In the evenings Hanka spun by the stove, his father patched harness, and Jaromir whittled spoons from a block of linden wood for the fair. Sometimes at night he was woken by a far-off whimpering howl. His wife, without opening her eyes, would feel for his hand in the dark and hold it until he fell asleep again. # pokaz: skowyt
At Candlemas, on the second of February, they went to Bukowiec by sledge. His mother insisted they buy a Candlemas candle – a thick taper of beeswax, which the priest blessed that day before the altar.
~ ekwipunek += gromnica
“Every house must have a Candlemas candle,” she said. “For storms, for childbirth and for dying.” # pokaz: gromnica
Hanka blushed to the tips of her ears, and only on the way home did Jaromir understand why. As the sledge drew in among the first cottages of Zapadlina, his wife took his hand and laid it on her belly. # dalej
-> przestrogi

= przestrogi
# tlo: przestrogi
From then on all Zapadlina knew there would be a child in Jaromir’s house, and every woman in the village had a piece of good advice for Hanka. She must not look at a fire, or the little one would be born with a red birthmark. She must not walk under a washing line, or the cord would wind itself round the baby’s neck. She must not sit on a threshold, nor lend fire to anyone.
Hanka laughed at all of it, but she listened – and from each piece of advice took as much as she judged wise. She did not look at fires, she did not walk under washing lines, but when Mrs Wójcik’s stove went out in February, she carried embers over to her in a pot herself.
“The Good Lord will sooner punish me for a neighbour’s cold cottage than for lending fire,” she cut in, when Jaromir’s mother threw up her hands.
But it was her grandmother who said the most – the same thing every time, word for word, until Jaromir knew it by heart:
“When the child’s born, don’t leave it alone, not for a moment. Not by day, not by night. Till it’s christened it isn’t yours yet, it’s nobody’s. By the lake live the mamunas. Their own babes are born ugly and sickly, so they steal healthy ones from folk and leave their own in the cradle. Do you know what they wash at night?” she always added at the end. “Shrouds. For those who’ll die in the village before next spring. Over every shroud they call out someone’s name. Whoever overhears will know who’s to be buried – but better not to listen.”
{ korale_kaski == zatrzymane:
    ~ ekwipunek -= korale
    That spring Hanka found under the straw mattress a scrap of cloth holding Kaśka’s beads. It was so wet that water dripped from it. Without a word Jaromir carried the beads to the lake and flung them as far from the shore as he could. That night, at last, he slept in peace. # pokaz: korale
}
In summer, when the nights grew short and warm, a steady slapping began to carry from the lake – as if someone were doing the washing in the dark, beating it against a stone with a washing paddle. The village women crossed themselves at the sound and closed their shutters. # pokaz: kijanki # dalej
-> narodziny

= narodziny
# tlo: przyzba_swit
Hanka’s labour began at the end of August, at night, while the sheaves still stood in the fields. Her grandmother came before anyone had sent for her. She drove Jaromir out of the room along with his father and told them to wait on the ledge by the wall.
They waited all night. Jaromir heard Hanka’s cries, and each one drove into his chest like a nail. His father sat beside him, {noga_ojca == zlamana:leaning on his stick|his crutches propped against the wall}, and kept silent. Only once, just before dawn, did he lay a hand on the back of his son’s neck and say:
“Your mother screamed just the same. And then there was you.”
When the sky grew pale above the forest, a cry came from the room – thin, angry, utterly new. # pokaz: swit
“A lass,” said the grandmother, appearing in the doorway. She was wiping her hands on her apron and, for the first time since Jaromir had known her, smiling broadly. “Sound as a bell.” # pokaz: babka
They named her Maria – Marysia – because when things were at their worst, Hanka had vowed her to the Blessed Virgin. # dalej
-> kolyska

= kolyska
# tlo: izba_kolyska
~ zapiski += mamuny_1
The christening was set for the coming Sunday, and until then no one in the cottage was to sleep easy.
Jaromir had knocked the cradle together himself from ash boards and set it by the bed, close enough for Hanka to reach it with her hand. It still smelled of fresh wood, and Marysia smacked her lips in her sleep and clenched her little fists on the hem of her swaddling cloth. The grandmother went all round the room, muttering under her breath, sprinkled salt on the threshold and told them to shield the child with something.
“One way, but done properly,” she said. “The mamunas are cunning. Leave them a crack no wider than a hair and they’ll squeeze through.”
