# tlo: noc_mamun
The first nights passed quietly. On the last, between Saturday and Sunday, Jaromir was woken by the slapping of washing paddles – not by the lake this time, but right under the window.
He lay motionless, watching. The moon stood high, and in its light two shadows were moving about the room. By the cradle stood two women – tall and hunchbacked, with heads too big for their scrawny necks. Their hair stuck out every which way like dry twigs, and their rags of moss and bark trailed across the floor, leaving wet smears. One held in her arms something swaddled in a dirty shawl. The other kept reaching into the cradle with thin, knotty hands overgrown with moss – and snatching them back each time, as though she had been scalded.{ ekwipunek ? podkowa: They had not crossed the threshold with the horseshoe above it – they had come in through the window.} # pokaz: mamuny
{ ochrona_kolyski:
- wstazka_na_raczce:
    They shielded their faces from the red ribbon on the baby’s wrist.
- siekiera_pod_kolyska:
    They kept clear of the side of the cradle where the axe blade jutted out.
- else:
    The flame of the Candlemas candle trembled and leaned towards them, but did not go out.
}
One of them began to hum – softly, lullingly, without words, a tune no one in the village sang, and one that made Jaromir’s eyelids droop of their own accord. Hanka sat up in bed. Her eyes were open, but she looked straight through him, as if he were made of air. Slowly she reached out a hand – {ochrona_kolyski == wstazka_na_raczce:towards the knot on Marysia’s wrist|{ochrona_kolyski == siekiera_pod_kolyska:towards the haft of the axe|towards the flame, to snuff it out}}. # pokaz: hanka
# pytanie: What will Jaromir do?
*   [Throw himself between Hanka and the cradle.]
    ~ noc_mamun = zaslonil
    Jaromir sprang from the bed and covered the cradle with his own body. Something cold and wet raked down his back, as though someone had furrowed it with claws of ice. The mamunas shrieked – piercingly, like lapwings over a bog – and backed away towards the window. The one with the swaddled thing hissed at him, baring black gums, and in passing drew a wet hand across Hanka’s nightshirt. Then both vanished into the darkness. # pokaz: zaslonil
    Hanka sank back onto the pillow and slept on. In the morning she remembered nothing – but when she went to feed Marysia, the little one writhed and screamed with hunger. Hanka’s milk was gone.
    “They didn’t get the child, so they took the milk,” said the grandmother. “You’ve bought yourselves off cheap, though it mightn’t seem so to you now.”
    Hanka did not let herself weep for long. That very day she begged a goat from Staś’s family, and from then on she fed Marysia from a horn, drop by drop, day and night. # dalej
