= chrzest
# tlo: chrzest
In the morning they found on the windowsill the thing swaddled in the dirty shawl, dropped by the mamunas as they fled. When the grandmother unwrapped it, inside was only a piece of birch log into which someone had carved a human face.
The grandmother spat and had it burned in the stove. The wood hissed and squealed before it caught.
On Sunday they christened Marysia in the little church in Bukowiec. Inside it smelled of incense and damp boards, and shafts of morning sun fell through the small windows. The godfather was Antek, who throughout the rite kept kneading his brother’s cap in his fingers, and the godmother old Mrs Wójcik, who held the baby as carefully as if she were made of glass.{ korale_kaski == oddane: Around Mrs Wójcik’s neck Jaromir saw the familiar rowan-berry beads – dry and dull, just like any others.} When the priest poured water over her little head, Marysia did not cry, but furrowed her brow and gave him such a stern look that Antek snorted with laughter and had to pretend he was coughing. # pokaz: woda # dalej
-> skarb_z_bazy

= odmieniec
# tlo: odmieniec
~ zapiski += odmieniec_1
The baby in the cradle looked like Marysia, but it was not her.
It was heavier than it should have been, and cold to the touch despite the featherbed. The skin on its back wrinkled and flaked like bark. It never cried – it only ate, without end. Hanka fed it until she was reeling with weariness, and afterwards it lay quiet, staring up at the ceiling beams with eyes darker than any child’s. Sometimes, when it thought no one was looking, it smiled like an old woman. # pokaz: oczy
The grandmother looked it over only once and drew back from the cradle.
“That’s not yours,” she said. “It’s a changeling.”
Hanka shuddered at the words and burst into tears.
“It can still be undone,” the grandmother went on. “But you must be quick about it, before seven days are out. After that the mamunas will take Marysia so far away that no power on earth will give her back.”
# pytanie: What will Jaromir do?
* [Do as the grandmother advises – carry the changeling out to the crossroads and pretend to beat it.]
    ~ sposob_na_odmienca = rozstaje
    # tlo: rozstaje
    At dusk Jaromir carried the changeling out to the crossroads below the forest and laid it on the ground. His hands were shaking. He raised a birch switch and lashed the earth beside it, again and again, so that it whistled through the air, and the changeling began to bawl – not like a child, but hoarsely, like a crow. # pokaz: rozga
    Out of the mist over the meadow ran a mamuna. She snatched her child up from the ground and clutched it to her breast – but instead of giving Marysia back, she retreated into the mist, cackling. # pokaz: mamuna
    “We give nothing back for free,” she croaked. “Come to the lake, goodman. After midnight.” # dalej
    -> nad_jeziorem_mamun
