    ~ sposob_na_odmienca = skorupki
    It was written in the book that a changeling who sees something it has never seen in all its long life will forget itself and speak in its own voice.
    Jaromir set out a row of empty eggshells on the iron plate of the stove and began pouring water into them. The changeling stopped eating. It propped itself up on its elbows in the cradle, as no infant can, and said in an old woman’s rasping voice: # pokaz: skorupki
    “I’m as old as the forest, and never yet have I seen anyone boil water in eggshells.”
    Hanka cried out. The window flew open of itself, a cold wind from the lake burst into the room – and when it died down, Marysia lay in the cradle, red from crying, her little fists clenched. # pokaz: wiatr # dalej
    -> skarb_z_bazy

= nad_jeziorem_mamun
# tlo: jezioro_mamun
After midnight Jaromir went down to the lake alone. Hanka saw him only as far as the gate.
“Come back with her,” she said. “Or don’t come back at all.”
At once she covered her mouth with her hand, frightened by her own words.
On the flat stones at the water’s edge knelt mamunas – several of them – beating wet rags with their washing paddles. At the sight of him they stopped. The eldest, so bent that her chin nearly touched her knees, rose slowly. In her arms she held something swaddled, from which came a faint whimpering. # pokaz: najstarsza
“A good child,” she croaked. “Warm. Healthy. Why should we give it back?”
{ sposob_na_odmienca == chrzest_odmienca:
    “You turned ours to splinters,” hissed another. “For that you’ll pay double.”
}
“What do you want?” asked Jaromir.
“Give us something you love,” answered the eldest. “Something you can never buy back.”
# pytanie: What will Jaromir give the mamunas?
*   [Give them his childhood treasure.]
    ~ uklad_z_mamunami = oddany_skarb
    Jaromir reached inside his shirt. Before setting out he had taken from the treasure chest in the den {skarb_jaromira == muszelka:the white shell|{skarb_jaromira == pioro:the jay’s feather|the rusty nail}} – for courage, as once on every childhood adventure. He had not imagined it would serve any other purpose.
    The mamuna sniffed the treasure like a dog, grinned with her black gums and hurled it into the lake. The ring on the water spread for a long time, until it reached his boots. # pokaz: skarb
    “A child’s happiness,” she croaked with satisfaction. “The first, the sweetest. Good payment.”{ sposob_na_odmienca == chrzest_odmienca: “And for ours…” added another, drawing an icy finger down his cheek. It left a thin, livid mark, as if from the lash of a switch.}
*   [Promise them a gift every year, on Midsummer Night.]
    ~ uklad_z_mamunami = dar_swietojanski
    “Every year, on Midsummer Night, I’ll leave you a loaf of bread and a wreath of rue on this stone,” said Jaromir. # pokaz: dar
    The mamunas whispered among themselves like reeds in the wind.
    “Every year,” the eldest repeated. “As long as you live.{ sposob_na_odmienca == chrzest_odmienca: And two loaves, not one.} And if you forget even once, we’ll come for what’s ours.”
*   [Give them his own years.]
    ~ uklad_z_mamunami = oddane_lata
    “Take my years,” said Jaromir. “Only give her back to me.”
    The eldest mamuna touched his temple. Her fingers were cold and soft as sodden bark. He felt something flowing out of him – slowly, painfully, like blood from a wound – and his legs buckled. # pokaz: lata
    “{sposob_na_odmienca == chrzest_odmienca:Fourteen|Seven},” she said, withdrawing her hand. “That will do.”
    When, gasping for breath, he bent over the water, he saw in it the face of a man with greying temples.
- The eldest mamuna handed him the swaddled child. In the shawl lay Marysia – warm, healthy, tear-stained. She smelled of silt and sweet flag, and in her clenched little fist she held a stalk of bulrush and would not let anyone take it from her. # pokaz: oddana
    When he raised his head, the stones were empty; only the water by the shore rocked a moment longer, as if someone had just stepped into it. # dalej
